<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Bhutan</h1><p class="st-meta">1907 CE – present · capital Thimphu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Himalayan Buddhist monarchy of the Wangchuck dynasty, known for its policy of Gross National Happiness.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After decades of civil strife in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zhabdrung-bhutan">Zhabdrung Bhutan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugyen-wangchuck">Ugyen Wangchuck</a>, penlop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trongsa">Trongsa</a>, defeated his rivals and gained British favour. In 1907 an assembly of monks, officials and nobles at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punakha">Punakha</a> elected him the first hereditary king, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druk-gyalpo">Druk Gyalpo</a>, ending the dual system of rule.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-punakha">Treaty of Punakha</a> in 1910 placed foreign affairs under British guidance, a role later taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1949. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jigme-dorji-wangchuck">Jigme Dorji Wangchuck</a> created a national assembly in 1953 and began modernisation, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thimphu">Thimphu</a> became the capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jigme-singye-wangchuck">Jigme Singye Wangchuck</a> promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-national-happiness">Gross National Happiness</a> and preserved traditional culture. The expulsion of many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhotshampa">Lhotshampa</a> of Nepali descent in the early 1990s remains controversial.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jigme-singye-wangchuck">Jigme Singye Wangchuck</a> devolved power and abdicated in 2006 in favour of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jigme-khesar-namgyel-wangchuck">Jigme Khesar Namgyel Wangchuck</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zhabdrung-bhutan">Bhutan</a> held its first parliamentary elections and adopted a constitution in 2008, becoming a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-bhutan.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Bhutan" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bhutan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1907 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Thimphu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ugyen Wangchuck, Jigme Singye Wangchuck, Jigme Khesar Namgyel Wangchuck</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Drukpa Kagyu Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dzongkha</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bhutan">Bhutan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bhutan, Druk Yul, Wangchuck dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1966&amp;focus=kingdom-of-bhutan" aria-label="Kingdom of Bhutan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7035 1170 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Bhutan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7355 1426L7359 1421M7355 1426L4997 651M7355 1426L7018 1386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7359 1421h0M4997 651h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7355 1426h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7355 1426h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Thimphu. <a href="/atlas?year=1966&amp;focus=kingdom-of-bhutan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
